message.content:
Below you’ll find two ways to get exactly what you asked for –  
* **Option A** uses the popular `async` npm‑package (`async.queue`) which already implements all the plumbing you described.  
* **Option B** shows how to build a tiny “concurrency‑aware” queue yourself so you don’t have to add another dependency.

Both examples give you:
| Feature | How it’s handled |
|---------|------------------|
| Concurrency 5 | Queue workers run up to five at once |
| Tasks are functions that log something | Each queued item is a zero‑argument function that prints its own message |
| Saturated / Unsaturated callbacks | When the number of running jobs reaches the concurrency cap → *saturated*, otherwise → *unsaturated* |

---

### Option A – Use `async.queue`

```bash
npm install async          # only needs to be done once per project
```

#### Code

```js
// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Import the helper
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const async = require('async');

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Create a queue with concurrency 5
// ------------------------------------------------------------
const q = async.queue((taskFn, cb) => {
  // Every job receives a single argument – our "function" that does the real work
  try {
    taskFn();            // <-- this will print whatever the caller wanted
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(err);
  }
  cb();                  // signal completion
}, /* concurrency */ 5);

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Hook into saturation & unsaturation events
// ------------------------------------------------------------
q.on('saturated', () => console.log('Queue is saturated'));
q.on('unsaturated', () => console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Push some demo tasks
// ------------------------------------------------------------
for (let i = 1; i <= 12; ++i) {
  const msg = `Task #${i}`;
  q.push(
    () => console.log(msg),      // ← the actual work
    err => {
      if (err) console.error(err);
    }
  );
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 5️⃣  Wait until everything finishes before exiting
// ------------------------------------------------------------
q.drain(() => console.log('All tasks processed!'));
```

> **What happens under the hood?**  
> - As soon as you call `push`, the queue checks whether there are fewer than 5 active workers. If yes, it starts one immediately.  
> - Once the count hits 5, the `'saturated'` listener fires.  
> - Whenever a worker completes (`cb()` inside the iterator), the queue may drop below 5 again, triggering `'unsaturated'`.  

Feel free to replace the loop with your real workload – just remember to pass a *zero‑arg function* that performs the desired side effect.

---

### Option B – Build a lightweight queue yourself

If you prefer no dependencies, here’s a minimal implementation that mimics the same behaviour:

```js
class SimpleQueue {
  constructor(concurrency) {
    this.concurrency = concurrency;
    this.running     = 0;
    this.tasks       = [];
    this.isSaturated = false;
  }

  /** Add a new task */
  enqueue(fn) {
    this.tasks.push(fn);
    process.nextTick(this._run.bind(this));
  }

  _run() {
    while (this.running < this.concurrency && this.tasks.length > 0) {
      const fn = this.tasks.shift();
      this.running++;

      // Fire saturated hook if needed
      if (!this.isSaturated && this.running === this.concurrency) {
        this.isSaturated = true;
        if (typeof this.onSaturated === 'function')
          this.onSaturated();
      }

      Promise.resolve()
        .then(fn)
        .catch(console.error)
        .finally(() => {
          this.running--;

          // Fire unsaturated hook if needed
          if (this.isSaturated && this.running < this.concurrency) {
            this.isSaturated = false;
            if (typeof this.onUnsaturated === 'function')
              this.onUnsaturated();
          }

          // Continue processing remaining items
          if (this.tasks.length || this.running) return;
          if (typeof this.onDrain === 'function') this.onDrain();
        });
    }
  }

  /** Convenience helpers for attaching listeners */
  on(eventName, handler) {
    switch (eventName) {
      case 'saturated':   this.onSaturated   = handler; break;
      case 'unsaturated': this.onUnsaturated = handler; break;
      case 'drain':       this.onDrain       = handler; break;
    }
  }
}

/*** Usage ***/
const queue = new SimpleQueue(5);

queue.on('saturated',   () => console.log('Queue is saturated'));
queue.on('unsaturated', () => console.log('Queue is unsaturated'));
queue.on('drain',       () => console.log('All tasks finished'));

for (let i = 1; i <= 12; ++i) {
  const id = i;
  queue.enqueue(() => console.log(`Running Task #${id}`));
}
```

This hand‑rolled version behaves almost identically to `async.queue`:  
* It keeps track of how many workers are currently busy (`running`).  
* When the count equals the configured concurrency, it calls the `"saturated"` callback.  
* When the count drops back below the limit, it triggers `"unsaturated"`.  
* Finally, when nothing remains to do, it emits `"drain"`.

Because it relies solely on native promises and `process.nextTick`, it works everywhere modern JS runs—no extra packages required.

---

## Quick recap

| What you wanted | Implementation |
|-----------------|---------------|
| Concurrency 5 | `new SimpleQueue(5)` or `async.queue(..., 5)` |
| Tasks that log | Pass a zero‑arg function such as `() => console.log("…")` |
| Saturation hooks | `.on('saturated', …)` / `.on('unsaturated', …)` |
| Drain notification | `.on('drain', …)` |

Pick whichever approach feels right for your stack. Happy queuing!

